    <string name="note_no_source_flags">The bits a charger uses to describe itself - mains powered, dual-role, USB capable - are not here: MediaTek\'s port controller decodes each object into four figures on the way to userspace and the flags are not among them.</string>
    <string name="note_request_position">This is the object the last request named, which is all this interface publishes of it - the voltage and current asked against it are not in sysfs anywhere on this platform. What is measured below is what is actually arriving.</string>
    <string name="note_request_programmable">The contract is against a programmable supply, so the voltage is whatever was last asked for within that object\'s range rather than a figure on the menu - the charging stack says so, and the request itself is not published. What is measured below is what is actually arriving.</string>
    <string name="note_adapter">The charging stack\'s own reading of the negotiation rather than anything on the wire. The handshake comes from the policy engine; the rest is the vendor\'s, and drives the charging animation as much as anything electrical.</string>
    <string name="note_measured_not_agreed">This is what is being delivered rather than what was agreed: nothing on this platform publishes the negotiated figures, only which object they were agreed against.</string>
    <string name="note_no_contract">Something is attached and the port controller has no capabilities from it, which is what a cable that never negotiated looks like: no source capabilities message ever arrived, so there is no list to show. A charger that speaks power delivery fills this in within a second of being plugged in.</string>
    <string name="note_no_object_interface">MediaTek\'s port controller could not be read, so the object list is out of reach. It is at /sys/class/tcpc, and on a ROM build it needs a label the app is allowed to read - see the app\'s README. The charger below still says what it settled on.</string>
    <string name="note_unavailable">No power delivery interface was found. The object list is MediaTek\'s own, at /sys/class/tcpc, and on this board it is either absent or not readable by this app.</string>
    <string name="note_unavailable_not_mediatek">No power delivery interface was found, and this does not look like a MediaTek board. This app reads MediaTek\'s port controller class at /sys/class/tcpc; a qualcomm handset wants QcomPdInfo instead.</string>

    <string name="root_needed">Root access is needed to read these files on this ROM, and it was not granted.</string>
